hi there steve kaufman here um  today i want to talk primarily about how  i learn words because that was a request  i had from one of the people who follows  me here at youtube how do i learn words  um  first of all before i get into that let  me just say that i've been experimenting  with using google plus hangouts  for discussions with uh primarily with  our link learners to explain some of the  functions of the new uh  you know new version of link linked to  2.0 and so forth and i found the  experience extremely frustrating uh it  may be because i'm technically inept but  it seems that  i go to invite people who come who sign  up they want to be there and then they  don't get invited or it's just been a  mess uh the link page at google plus it  seems that there's like  four or five or six postings of the same  hangout i don't know what's going on  and i must say that in general i find  this whole world of twitter facebook  google plus linkedin  whatever  uh i mean i only use it as a means of  trying to spread the word about link i  certainly don't follow anyone there  um  you know after this  little video i'm going to settle down  with a book i have a spanish hangout  tomorrow at 8 so i'm going to read a  book in spanish i haven't quite decided  what i have a book on spanish history i  have  el sor sombrero de tres picos which i  have never read and then there's a book  about the functioning of the brain in  spanish i'll choose one of them and  settle down for a couple of hours in  peace and quiet and just to get away  from this uh all this constant chirping  that comes at you on these uh  on these social sites but that's another  issue words okay to me language learning  is primarily about acquiring words  i firmly believe that  and that is why at link the most  important  uh statistic that we track is the number  of words you know  we don't necessarily think the number is  accurate  scientific but it's an indication of  your relative improvement in the  language  now let me backtrack a bit i can still  remember when i i hitchhiked on a boat a  small german  uh  [ __ ] steamer going from montreal to  europe a 19  whatever it was 62 62-63 i found work i  just two three days i went down to the  docks of montreal and i asked if i could  work my way across the atlantic and  finally there was this one  german trap steamer they'd lost a sailor  in quebec city and they said fine  and i still remember sitting there in my  cabin at night going over these  declension tables for german  hoping that somehow that would help me  to speak  and it really didn't  um  and of course then i studied in france  and i didn't do any of that sort of  stuff with french mind you i had had  french in school but mostly when i was  studying i was reading books because i  had to i was at university in french i  had to write essays in french i had to  read tons of stuff in in in french of  course i had a dictionary hand in i was  looking up words  uh so then the next language that i went  after was chinese where i was assigned  by the canadian  trade commissioner service to learn  chinese  and there again what i did was  in order to learn words i only  read  books which had glossaries i refused to  go after any material where i had to use  a dictionary because it was so time  consuming  and of course we all know that any word  we look up we're going to forget right  away so it was a tremendously  frustrating to spend all this time  counting strokes or going by the uh what  they call the radical  component of the chinese character in  order to find this character in order to  find out what it  meant i just went with glossaries and i  did better than  anyone else who was studying chinese at  that time in hong kong and i believe  it's because i read so much i read and  read and read i had a huge library every  book that was put out  on on in chinese with glossaries i had  it and i'd read it until i got to the  point after seven or eight months where  i actually could read without the  glossaries  so  i'm kind of leading to how you acquire  words which was the question you acquire  words through input in my opinion  um again  at link now link to my mind is the most  efficient way to acquire words you don't  need the glossaries the problem with the  glossary is  that it's kind of behind the chapter or  at the back of the book so you come to a  word you don't know you look it up in  the glossary half the time it isn't  there but on the other hand there's a  bunch of words in that glossary that you  already know in other words the person  who wrote the book can't anticipate  which words you know in which words you  don't know nowadays with online  dictionaries you don't have to do that  you can read the online dictionary gives  you the word right away that's not to  say that you know the word because you  looked it up you don't  the  definitions you find in the dictionary  may not even suit the context that  you're reading but it gives you a bit of  a half a hint and then you encounter  with the word again and again and again  and each time you get a better sense of  how that word is used  and of course we do this at link and  every time you say a word is highlighted  in yellow and that reminds you that you  once looked it up and then you go in and  look at the definition definition again  because you've forgotten that you might  add to the definition adjust it or  whatever  but the surprising thing is  that in both russian and czech where i  have very good statistics  i have learned more words like i know  more words that i never looked up  than  those words that i looked up so if i  have saved 30 000 links at link  maybe 20 000 words and 10 000 phrases or  25 000 words and 10 000 phrases but um  our statistics say that i have 75 000  words whatever that statistic means but  i know for a fact that nowadays if i  import a text from a russian book or a  russian website you know there's five  percent words that i don't know there  very few words that i don't know so i  have learned an awful lot of words most  of them i have learned  through listening and reading i am a  very sporadic flash card i don't do it  regularly only because i don't have the  time i have an hour an hour and a half a  day  and not every day to spend on language  learning so i'm going to do things that  i enjoy doing which means reading and  listening i'm not going to sit down and  flip through flashcards most of the time  if i had six or seven hours then  obviously it's good to vary the kind of  activity you're engaged in so then i  might spend more time flashcarding so to  me  overwhelmingly you learn words through  listening and reading  and there are little things you can do  to help you  personally i think the yellow  highlighting in our texts the way we do  at link helps you  i will occasionally look at a word and  see all the examples that we have  because that helps me see how that word  is used because there are words that you  see  and you always seem to have only a 60  sense of how that word is used and you  would like to nail it down even the  dictionary definition doesn't nail it  down for you but if i see 10 examples of  how that word is used in different  contexts that starts you know it moves  me along because after all you're  constantly kind of moving from that  initial exposure to the word  to where you finally are confident that  you know how that word is used  so i learn words listening and reading  now there are people who say you can't  claim that you know a word until you can  use it  i don't agree  all right  i feel that your passive vocabulary will  always exceed your active vocabulary in  a foreign language by quite an amount  because if you're talking to native  speakers they're going to have a much  bigger vocabulary than you do and you  have to be able to understand what  they're saying you can choose  from a more limited range of words to  express your thoughts but you need a  much larger vocabulary to make sure you  understand what they're saying similarly  if you're reading books if you're  watching movies  if you're listening to a radio program  you need a larger passive vocabulary to  make sure you understand so it's normal  you have a smaller active vocabulary and  the passive vocabulary is basically the  foundation for the active vocabulary  you're not likely to have an active word  you know part of your active vocabulary  that isn't already in your passive  vocabulary it starts in the passive to  my mind everything starts with  understanding if you can understand you  can learn to speak and the way to  acquire words is to focus on  understanding focus on listening and  reading and just  listen and read as much as you possibly  can and obviously to speak well you have  to speak a lot but until as i've said  before until you have enough words and  enough  of a sense of familiarity with the  language any conversation you're going  to have is so limited that i consider to  be not a meaningful conversation the  kind of conversation you can only have  with a tutor or a friend  and personally  i would rather spend an hour or two that  i have a day  engaging with more meaningful content  even it's if it's passively listening  and reading rather than having some kind  of a make conversation with someone just  so that i can practice saying the  limited things that i know how to say in  the language i would rather wait until  i'm able to have a meaningful  conversation and that's what i do in  czech and and in russian we have  meaningful conversations we talk about  politics we talk about history we talk  about things that are going on in canada  or in those countries and those are  meaningful conversations and that to me  is where i want to get to but i begin  with this focus on understanding and  learning words and overwhelmingly i  learn them through massive listening and  reading so whoever it was who asked me  how do you learn words  that's how you learn words  and you say okay well you can't read if  you don't know the words that's right  you start with small  short  content items  preferably where you also have the audio  to reinforce it  you have some way of looking up the  words you don't know  and keeping track of those words you can  do it with pencil and writing out lists  or you can do it the way we do at length  or you can transfer them to flash cards  the anki  whatever system you want but you have to  have some way  that you are  gradually increasing the length and the  level of sophistication of the content  you're listening to and somehow keeping  track of your accumulation of vocabulary  again there are people who say that you  should go use you know graded readers  and always have this uh you know  95  of the words should be known to you this  sort of n plus one only slightly sort of  too difficult for me that's too slow  because if i did that in check  you know i would still be reading  kitty's stories in check so i tend to  spend a couple of months to get to a  certain level and then they jump right  into difficult stuff slog my way through  where there's 30 40 percent unknown  words and i save those words in linkedin  and we'll review them and then listen  and read again and listen and read again  and slowly i just keep grinding through  and because the actual  content is interesting to me  i i have a great sense of relation that  i'm reading about czech history if it's  czech or or politics and now i'm into  korean and so basically that's it you  you fight your way through initially uh  initially very simple and uninteresting  material as quickly as possible you  hopefully get into interesting material  but with a high level of unknown words  you fight your way through those reading  and listen you know listening and  reading and listening until there's  fewer and fewer unknown words there and  then gradually it becomes more and more  pleasant and and you're on your way and  the thing about words is that the more  words you know  the more words you can infer and the  more you know the more interesting  content you can expose yourself to and  therefore acquire more and more words so  it's a bit of a snowball so there you  have it on acquiring words i hope that  answers the question thank you bye for  now

